[Music]  try to go back in time and imagine  yourself as a baby I'm not gonna ask you  to start crying or crawling around the  room but try to think of the major  learnings you had perhaps it was  learning how to walk or how to hold an  object by yourself maybe it was learning  how not to heat your baby friends or  even how to say her first words but let  me ask you by a show of hands how many  of you were taught how to see no one  right you shouldn't blame your parents  for that my point here is that human  sight is usually taken for granted  unless you lose it for some reason or  unless you're born with an eye  impairment which was my case too your  friendly eyes it might seem that I don't  have any problem but I was born blind  from my left eye and I have low vision  in my right one so I'd like to welcome  you to my world that's probably a very  good approximation of how I see things  in this case this is not a thing but  actually the love of my life on our  wedding day but the fact is that since I  was a kid I had to understand how to go  through that blur I had to learn how to  see now you might be asking what  learning how to see looks like what is  seeing well biology has an explanation  for us it says that it's all about light  light that goes through a series of very  delicate structures in our I like the  cornea  the pupil until it finally reaches our  retina a photosensitive tissue that  transforms light into electrical signals  those electrical signals then are  processed and travel from our eye to our  brain so in fact our eyes just collect  visual information but we see with our  brain according to science vision is an  activity 90% mental  and only 10% physical so when I look  back into my struggles I see now that  biology is indeed right and the act of  seeing is much more in here and maybe in  here than in here I truly believed that  real sight is based on three main actors  completely unrelated to our physical  condition and I hope to share them with  you today through some of the lessons  that I had in my life my first lesson  came pretty early at the age of four  trying to help me be part of the world  in a different way my parents decided to  put me in a music school and before even  learning how to read words by  transforming those little black  bloodstains into music notes I was able  to see a brand-new world through sound  but seeing that world required a lot of  effort a lot of practice and a lot of  dedication so I learned that seeing is  actually about the level of death you  put in everything you do in your life my  second lesson came some years later than  that I was 17 years old I was very shy  probably facing a lot of the same  challenges you've all faced at that age  but I had this crazy idea of auditioning  to a theatre company  well crazier than me were the ones who  accepted an actor who could barely see  to the other side of the stage but at  that same stage with a bunch of light in  my face  I learned that seeing is much more about  perspective by looking to the world  through the eyes of many many different  characters I learned how different  people are and yet how similar we are to  each other my third and final lesson  came more recently I've always been  passionate about photography  maybe because images were always a bit  mysterious and blurred to me I couldn't  see them well but I never thought I  would be able to use a professional  camera to take professional photographs  well recently I met a photographer and  he told me I have you ever noticed that  you only need one eye to look through  the viewfinder of a camera  indeed his right so this picture you're  seeing here was taken by a photographer  with just one eye you're looking at him  right now so through my photographs I  also learned that vision is much more  about focusing in your dreams focusing  in what matters most to you and why  I truly believe that all of those  lessons should be taught to us together  with that biology class I just showed  you some slides ago and I'm sure that  you all have already experienced at  least one moment in your lives where you  didn't use even those 10% to actually  see the world don't you remember that  very first case when you almost  automatically automatically close your  eyes I'm sure you're very very focused  at that moment how about the dozens of  times your friends asked you to close  your eyes just to hit you with a  surprise from a brand new perspective  not to mention watching a concert where  your favorite rock star closes his eyes  at the peak of the song I'm sure he was  seeing a lot of stuff in that moment and  experiencing the deepest emotions Helen  Keller was an American educator and  activist for the blind and the deaf  people she was blind actually and she  once said what I'm looking for is not  out there  it's in me so I'd like to invite you to  step into my world even if you have  perfect sight so the next time you face  a challenge a hard decision or even a  simple pleasuring life try not to look  at it only with your eyes wide open  close them and open up your brain so you  can unlock a different kind of vision  one that goes beyond that blur and helps  you be more focused broaden your  perspectives and look deeper inside  thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

